_⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_

**Side effects and `RestoreGlobals` component definition in the render body introduce two correctness risks.**

**1. Side effects executed during render (lines 66–69)**

`spyOn`, `mockClear`, and the global mutation all run in the render body of the decorator function, which React treats as a render-phase call. In React Strict Mode, render functions are invoked twice. Each call to `spyOn(console, 'error')` stacks a new spy wrapping the previous one; the single `mockRestore()` in the cleanup then only unwraps the innermost wrapper, leaving the previous spy in place. Moving the setup into a `useEffect` (with the same cleanup) eliminates the double-invocation risk and keeps side effects out of the render phase.

**2. `RestoreGlobals` defined inline creates an unstable component type (lines 71–80)**

A new function reference for `RestoreGlobals` is produced on every render of the decorator. React compares component types by reference to decide whether to reuse or remount a subtree. Because the reference changes every render, React will unmount and immediately remount `RestoreGlobals` on any re-render of the decorator, firing the cleanup `useEffect` prematurely—restoring globals and the spy before the story is finished.

The fix is to move `RestoreGlobals` outside the decorator (or outside the `meta` block entirely) so its identity is stable, and to move the setup side effects into a `useEffect` within it.



<details>
<summary>🔧 Proposed fix</summary>

```diff
+// Stable component defined at module scope to prevent remounting on re-renders
+const RestoreGlobals = ({ children }: { children: React.ReactNode }) => {
+  useEffect(() => {
+    const spy = spyOn(console, 'error').mockImplementation(() => {});
+    consoleErrorSpy = spy;
+    sendTelemetryErrorMock.mockClear();
+    const originalSendTelemetryError = globalThis.sendTelemetryError;
+    globalThis.sendTelemetryError = sendTelemetryErrorMock;
+    return () => {
+      spy.mockRestore();
+      globalThis.sendTelemetryError = originalSendTelemetryError;
+    };
+  }, []);
+  return <>{children}</>;
+};
+
 decorators: [
   (Story) => {
-    consoleErrorSpy = spyOn(console, 'error').mockImplementation(() => {});
-    sendTelemetryErrorMock.mockClear();
-    const originalSendTelemetryError = globalThis.sendTelemetryError;
-    globalThis.sendTelemetryError = sendTelemetryErrorMock;
-
-    const RestoreGlobals = ({ children }: { children: React.ReactNode }) => {
-      useEffect(
-        () => () => {
-          consoleErrorSpy.mockRestore();
-          globalThis.sendTelemetryError = originalSendTelemetryError;
-        },
-        []
-      );
-      return <>{children}</>;
-    };
-
     return (
       <RestoreGlobals>
         <Story />
       </RestoreGlobals>
     );
   },
 ],
```

> Note: With this approach `consoleErrorSpy` is set inside the effect, so play functions (which run after mount) will always see the initialized value.

</details>

